@@s001 Threads, states, scheduler
@@s002 ora:current state,dato:transition just made
@@s003 <p><code>new Turnstile()</code>: the object exists, but the thread has not started yet.</p>
@@s004 <p><code>start()</code>: the thread becomes <b>enabled</b>, that is ready. It is not yet executing instructions.</p>
@@s005 <p>The <b>scheduler</b> chooses it: now it is <b>running</b> and executes instructions. When this happens is not decided by the program.</p>
@@s006 <p>The scheduler <b>suspends</b> it at any moment, even halfway through <code>counter++</code>, and lets another thread work. It returns to enabled.</p>
@@s007 <p>Chosen again: running again.</p>
@@s008 <p>The thread makes a <b>blocking</b> call (<code>join()</code>, <code>lock()</code> with the lock taken, <code>await()</code>…): it moves to <b>blocked</b>. Here the transition is caused by the code of the thread.</p>
@@s009 <p>The expected event happens (the other thread ends, the lock is released…). The thread returns to <b>enabled</b>, not running: it must wait for the scheduler again.</p>
@@s010 <p>Chosen again, it runs until the end of <code>run()</code>.</p>
@@s011 <p><code>run()</code> has finished: the thread terminates. Whoever called <code>join()</code> on it can resume.</p>
@@s012 dato:main thread,due:turnstile thread,ora:running,ok:result
@@s013 dato:t1 running,due:t2 running
@@s014 Threads, states, scheduler
@@s015 A thread is not always executing: it passes between some states, and who decides when is the <b>scheduler</b>, in a non-deterministic way. From this freedom come the interleavings, and so all the problems of the course.
@@s016 The Tivoli turnstile
@@s017 A <b>thread</b> is a flow of instructions that advances on its own: "an independent sequence of program statements". Every Java program starts with one thread, <code>main</code>; others are created with <code>new Thread(…)</code> and start with <code>start()</code>. All the threads of a program see the same fields of the objects: that is <b>shared memory</b>, the way they communicate, and the source of the problems of the course. <code>join()</code> makes a thread wait until another has finished.
@@s018 The example of the lecture: a thread simulates 10 000 people who enter through a turnstile and increment a shared counter.
@@s019 CounterThreads.java (simplified)
@@s020 <b>Shared memory</b>: <code>counter</code> is a field, visible to the main thread and to the turnstile. <code>final</code> on <code>PEOPLE</code> says that it will never change.
@@s021 The main thread creates the turnstile and starts it.
@@s022 It waits for it to finish.
@@s023 The behaviour of the turnstile: 10 000 increments.
@@s024 With <b>one</b> turnstile it always prints <code>10000 people entered</code>: no other thread touches <code>counter</code> while it works. With two turnstiles things change: <a href="l01-interleaving.html">next page</a>.
@@s025 main and turnstile on the timeline
