@@s039 To remember at the oral
@@s040 From <b>blocked</b> you never go directly back to <b>running</b>: you always pass through <b>enabled</b>. You will find the same idea in monitors: a thread woken by <code>signal()</code> must compete again for the lock.
@@s041 Non-determinism
@@s042 Two threads <code>t1</code> and <code>t2</code>, both enabled. The scheduler can produce, for example:
@@s043 Practical consequence
@@s044 <b>No assumption</b> is allowed about which thread runs next. This is why a test that passes does not prove that a concurrent program is correct: you have seen one execution out of infinitely many. Correctness is argued from the <b>constraints</b> (mutual exclusion, happens-before), not from the executions observed.
@@s045 Three different executions of the same two threads
